e, Oberschleißheim, München"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/158610"} "80 from 20 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "East Germany sent its defending Olympic champions, " [:a {:href "/athletes/37597"} "Frank Forberger"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/37603"} "Dieter Grahn"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/37646"} "Frank Rühle"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37653"} "Dieter Schubert"] ", known as “The Dresden Four”, to the coxless fours events at the 1972 Games and, as they had not lost an international match since their 1968 victory, they were heavily favored. The Soviet quartet, however, had a faster time in the opening round, while the New Zealand crew defeated the East Germans by a margin of nearly three seconds in the semi-finals. It was quite possible, however, that East Germany was merely conserving its strength, for it had little trouble taking the gold medal in the final. The New Zealanders did not shy away from the challenge, however, and put forth a valiant struggle to best their European adversaries. Their efforts did not win them an Olympic title, but they did net the nation a silver medal, which they earned a comfortable distance ahead of the third-place West Germans."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "East Germany"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GDR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GDR.png"}] "GDR"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "8"} [:a {:href "/athletes/37597"} "Frank Forberger"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37603"} "Dieter Grahn"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37646"} "Frank Rühle"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37653"} "Dieter Schubert"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}